Minimal $ORIGIN-RPATH reproducer for the FEX_SMCCHECKS=full path-truncation bug.

BUILD:
  export PATH=/home/jbettcher/Development/fexrootfs/x-tools/x86_64-linux-gnu/bin:$PATH
  x86_64-linux-gnu-gcc -shared -fPIC lib.c -o liborigin.so
  x86_64-linux-gnu-gcc main.c -L. -lorigin -Wl,-rpath,'$ORIGIN' -o origin_test

RUN:
  # Baseline (default SMC) -- should print "f() = 42" exit 0
  FEX ./origin_test

  # SMC=full -- ld.so cannot find liborigin.so
  FEX_SMCCHECKS=full FEX ./origin_test

TRIGGER: only when the guest binary is loaded via a path where ld.so must
resolve $ORIGIN (RPATH/RUNPATH). Simple binaries like /bin/echo, /bin/ls,
/usr/bin/perl -e '...' all run cleanly under SMC=full because they don't
exercise the affected ld.so path.

OBSERVATION (via strace on the host FEX process): the openat path FEX passes
to the host kernel is truncated. The truncated OUTPUT length is 8-byte
aligned (or 8k+1). Truncation amount depends on input $ORIGIN length, with
inputs of the same length always producing the same output length.

Example: for /home/.../build-probes/game/ (57-char $ORIGIN) the truncation
drops "e" -> 56-char output ".../gam". For /home/.../build-probes/gameyzab/
(61-char $ORIGIN) it drops "eyzab" -> also 56-char output ".../gam".

Full experimental grid captured in docs/build-agent-notes.md under the
"minimal $ORIGIN reproducer" entry.

NOT a general RPATH bug -- confined to SMC=full mode. Baseline (mtrack) and
SMCCHECKS=none both handle the same binary correctly.
